Vibe coding is when you describe what you want in plain English and an AI writes the code; agentic AI is when an AI system can plan, use tools, take actions, check results, and complete a multi-step task with limited supervision. Vibe coding is mainly a way of creating software; agentic AI is a broader way of getting work done.

Vibe coding vs agentic AI
Aspect	Vibe coding	Agentic AI
Core idea	You prompt an AI to generate code	You give an AI a goal; it plans and executes steps
Human role	You describe, review, and guide continuously	You set the goal, constraints, and review checkpoints
AI role	Writes code from your prompt	Plans, calls tools, executes tasks, tests, and iterates
Typical output	A code snippet, webpage, script, or prototype	A completed workflow, report, ticket update, or multi-step task
Control	High human involvement	More autonomy, with human oversight
Best for	Fast prototyping and exploration	Repeatable, multi-step business workflows
For example, if you tell an AI, “Build me a simple expense-tracker web app,” and it writes the HTML, CSS, and JavaScript, that is vibe coding. If you tell an AI, “Find all unpaid invoices above ₹50,000, email the owners for approval, update the finance system, and create a summary report,” and it actually performs those steps using connected tools, that is agentic AI.

Agentic AI in simple words
An AI agent is like a capable assistant that does not merely answer questions—it can decide what to do next and use tools to do it.

An agent usually has:

A goal: “Prepare this month’s sustainability report.”

Memory: It remembers the conversation and prior steps.

Planning: It breaks the goal into smaller steps.

Tools: It can search a database, read files, call APIs, send emails, or update records.

Checking/iteration: It verifies whether the result is complete and corrects mistakes.

Human approval: For sensitive actions, a person reviews before the agent acts.

LangChain describes autonomous agents as combining tool calling, memory, and planning.

Example: ESG report agent
Imagine an Oren-style sustainability agent:

User: “Prepare our Q3 BRSR-ready energy and emissions report for the Pune plant.”

The agent may:

Check which data is missing.

Search the company’s utility-bill database.

Extract electricity consumption from invoices.

Ask the plant manager to approve uncertain values.

Calculate emissions using the correct emission factor.

Compare results with last quarter and flag unusual changes.

Generate a draft report with source links.

Send it to the sustainability lead for final approval.

The key point: the agent is not just chatting. It is taking actions across systems, while keeping a human in the loop for approvals and exceptions.

RAG pipeline
RAG means Retrieval-Augmented Generation.

A normal AI model answers from what it learned during training. RAG adds a step: before answering, the system first retrieves relevant documents or records from your company’s data, then gives those documents to the AI so its answer is based on them.

Simple RAG flow
User asks: “What was our Pune plant’s electricity consumption in Q3?”

Retrieve: The system searches company documents, invoices, and databases for relevant information.

Augment: It adds the retrieved passages to the AI’s prompt.

Generate: The AI writes an answer using those documents.

Cite: Ideally, the answer links to the exact invoice or source record.

LangChain describes this as retrieving relevant data at query time and passing it to the model along with the user question.

Example
Without RAG:

“Our Pune plant used approximately X kWh in Q3.”

That answer may be invented or outdated.

With RAG:

“Your Pune plant used 1,24,500 kWh in Q3, based on three utility invoices dated July, August, and September. The total emissions were 87.15 tonnes CO2e.”

The second answer is more useful because it is grounded in actual company data and can include sources. RAG helps reduce hallucination and makes answers specific to private company information.

RAG vs a chatbot
Feature	Normal chatbot	RAG chatbot
Knowledge source	General training knowledge	Company documents, databases, policies
Answer style	General	Specific and source-backed
Risk	May invent facts	Lower hallucination risk, but retrieval quality still matters
Example	“What is Scope 2?”	“What was our Scope 2 emissions last quarter?”
