MCP
MCP means Model Context Protocol. It is an open standard that lets AI applications connect securely to external systems—such as files, databases, tools, and business workflows.

Think of MCP as a universal adapter between an AI assistant and the software systems it needs to use.

Without MCP, every AI product may need a custom integration for every tool. With MCP, a system can expose standard “tools” and “data sources” that an AI application can discover and use.

Example
Suppose Oren builds an MCP server for its ESG platform. An AI assistant could then ask:

“Show me all suppliers with overdue Scope 3 data.”

Using MCP, the assistant can connect to Oren’s supplier database, retrieve the relevant records, and display them—without the user manually exporting a spreadsheet.

MCP could also connect an AI assistant to:

A company’s HR system, to retrieve employee training or safety data

A finance system, to retrieve energy or travel expenses

A document store, to retrieve sustainability policies

A ticketing system, to create follow-up tasks

A messaging system, to notify a plant manager

MCP’s official documentation describes it as a standard for connecting AI applications to data sources, tools, and workflows.

In one line: MCP is the standard plug/socket that lets an AI agent safely connect to external business systems.

