Important product caveat
For an ESG product, an agent should not be allowed to silently change compliance-critical numbers. The better design is:

AI extracts and suggests data.

The system validates it against rules.

A human reviews uncertain values.

Every accepted value retains a source and audit trail.

Only approved data enters the final report.

That is especially important because Oren’s value proposition is not just automation—it is trustworthy, traceable, and assurance-ready sustainability reporting.

Interview-ready explanation
If Anshul asks, “What do you understand by agentic AI and RAG?” say:

“RAG makes an AI answer specific questions by first retrieving relevant company data and then generating an answer grounded in that data. An agentic AI goes further: it can plan a multi-step task, decide which tools to use, retrieve or update data, handle exceptions, and seek human approval where needed. For an ESG platform, I would use RAG for answering questions from policies and reports, while an agent could manage the end-to-end reporting workflow—provided every compliance-critical value remains traceable and human-reviewable.”

That shows both technical understanding and the product judgement that matters for Oren.

Please can you do a research on the web and let me know valuable information only form this PM assessment point of view?


Yes. For this assessment, the most valuable research is not “learn everything about climate change.” It is to understand what makes cement plants vulnerable, which risks Meera can defend in a board meeting, and what data you can realistically show in v1.

The single most important insight
Your prototype should not simply say, “This plant has high climate risk.”

It should say:

“This plant has high climate exposure because it faces a specific hazard, depends on a critical input such as water or clinker, and has limited ability to absorb the impact.”

That is the difference between a generic dashboard and a useful product.

For Nimbus, exposure has three parts:

Climate Exposure
=
Hazard
×
Operational Dependency
×
Financial / Strategic Materiality
Climate Exposure=Hazard×Operational Dependency×Financial / Strategic Materiality
Example: a plant may face high heat, but if it has a small capacity and low asset value, it may not be the top priority. Conversely, a plant with moderate heat risk but high output, high emissions, and a captive coal plant may be a major priority.

Physical risk: what matters for cement
Cement is a resource-intensive industry, so the most relevant physical risks are water stress, extreme heat, and extreme precipitation/flooding. Industry climate-risk disclosures specifically identify these as material risks for cement companies.

Physical hazard	Why it matters to Nimbus	What to show in v1
Extreme heat	Worker safety, equipment cooling, productivity, and potential output disruption	Historical and projected hot days; compare 2026 with 2040
Water stress / drought	Cement production, dust suppression, cooling, and community relations depend on water	Water-stress rating, rainfall pattern, water-source dependence
Flood / extreme rain	Can disrupt site access, logistics, raw materials, and clinker movement	River-discharge indicator, flood-risk category, coastal exposure
Cyclones	Particularly relevant for coastal plants	Coastal flag, location context, disruption exposure
Gradual climate change	Long-term operating conditions may become less favourable	2040 trend rather than a single exact prediction
Physical risks can be acute—such as floods, storms, and heatwaves—or chronic, such as long-term temperature and rainfall changes.

Water is a major product angle
Do not treat water as just another chart. For Nimbus, water is an operational dependency.

The plant file tells you which plants use groundwater, reservoirs, rivers, canals, municipal supply, or desalinated seawater. That matters because:

Groundwater-dependent plants may face depletion, regulatory restrictions, or community conflict.

River/canal-dependent plants may face low flow, pollution, or allocation restrictions.

Coastal plants using desalinated seawater may be less water-stressed but more exposed to coastal hazards.

Municipal-supply plants may face supply reliability and cost risk.

WRI Aqueduct’s baseline water-stress indicator measures water withdrawals relative to available water; higher values mean more competition for water. Its drought-risk indicator estimates the likelihood that drought will affect people and infrastructure.

Product implication: Give Meera a “water dependency” view, not only a generic water-risk score.

Transition risk: what matters for cement
For cement, transition risk is mainly about emissions intensity, carbon costs, coal dependence, and decarbonisation investment.

India’s Carbon Credit Trading Scheme has notified emissions-intensity targets for cement. ICAP reports an average reduction target of 2.7% by 2027 for integrated cement, based on FY2024 baseline data, with legally binding targets for FY2026 and FY2027.

That means your transition-risk view should not be vague. It should show:

Transition factor	Why it matters	What to show
Scope 1 CO2	Direct emissions create carbon-cost exposure	Emissions per plant, emissions per tonne of output
Emissions intensity	A plant may be large but efficient, or small but inefficient	CO2 per tonne of cement / clinker
Carbon-price sensitivity	Shows what happens if carbon becomes costly	Slider: ₹500, ₹1,000, ₹2,000, ₹5,000 per tonne CO2
CCTS exposure	Shows regulatory pressure	Current intensity versus sector target direction
Coal dependence	Captive coal power increases transition exposure	Flag P01’s 40 MW captive coal plant
Asset age	Older kilns may need more investment	Flag P05’s 1998 kiln
Decarbonisation readiness	Shows available mitigation options	Waste-heat recovery, alternative fuels, renewable electricity, clinker substitution
Cement decarbonisation levers
Use these as the “recommended actions” in your prototype. They make your product feel industry-aware rather than generic.

The main cement decarbonisation levers are:

Reduce clinker intensity: Use supplementary cementitious materials such as fly ash, slag, and calcined clay to replace part of the clinker in cement.

Use alternative fuels: Replace some fossil fuels with waste-derived or biomass-based fuels.

Improve energy efficiency: Reduce thermal and electrical energy use.

Decarbonise electricity: Use renewable electricity and waste-heat recovery.

Deploy carbon capture: A longer-term option for unavoidable process emissions.

NITI Aayog estimates that green alternative fuels can reduce cement-sector emissions by 6–13%, while renewable electricity integration and waste-heat recovery can contribute another 6–10%.

Apply this to Nimbus
Plant observation	Relevant action	Why it matters
P01 has a 40 MW captive coal power plant	Coal-to-renewables transition plan	Reduces Scope 1 and transition exposure
P03 has a 30 MW waste-heat recovery system	Highlight as a transition-risk mitigant	Already using a decarbonisation lever
P05 has the oldest kiln, from 1998	Energy-efficiency and modernisation review	Older assets may have higher emissions intensity and upgrade needs
P06 is the newest kiln, from 2022	Lower immediate modernisation priority	Newer asset, but still high clinker production
P08 uses fly ash from nearby power plants	Highlight clinker-substitution opportunity	Reduces clinker intensity and emissions
P09 uses steel slag	Highlight slag-based cement opportunity	Uses industrial by-products and can reduce clinker demand
This is a strong product insight: P08 and P09 are not just low-emission grinding units; they may also be part of Nimbus’s decarbonisation strategy because they use industrial by-products.

Clinker logistics is a hidden risk
This is one of the best ways to stand out.

P01 ships clinker to P07 by rail. P06 ships clinker to P08 and P09. That means P07, P08, and P09 depend on upstream plants and transport routes.

So your prototype should show a dependency chain:

text
P01 Chittorgarh ──rail──> P07 Mundra
P06 Baloda Bazar ──> P08 Paradip
P06 Baloda Bazar ──> P09 Durgapur
If a flood, heatwave, rail disruption, or water shortage affects P01 or P06, it can affect the grinding units downstream.

This makes your product more realistic than a simple map of “plant risk.” It shows that climate risk can propagate through operations.

